CREATED:20250821T024741Z
DESCRIPTION:Jack Paine (Emory) will present\, "The Last of the Monarchies:
  How Colonial State Formation Reshaped Regime Type." \n\nAbstract:\nAs la
 te as the nineteenth century\, states were governed almost exclusively by
  monarchies. Today\, though\, ruling monarchies are rare. Focusing on Afr
 ica and Asia\, I locate this transformation in the process of forming and
  reshaping colonial states. Although the overwhelming majority of ninetee
 nth-century monarchies became Western dependencies\, less than one-third 
 of these monarchies were eliminated by the colonizer. Instead\, the main 
 challenge to monarchical hegemony was the colonizers' preference to agglo
 merate smaller monarchies into larger colonies. This was a dynamic proces
 s that unfolded across the colonial period\; colonial units were frequent
 ly reshaped even after World War II. Subnational monarchs were rarely abl
 e to successfully recover power in the face of rising nationalist pressur
 e and expanding electorates. The monarchies that survived as postcolonial
  national institutions were not the largest and strongest precolonial sta
 tes\, like the Sokoto Caliphate in Nigeria or the descendants of the Mugh
 al Empire in India. Instead\, colonial survivors were often small units w
 hich benefitted from an exceptional characteristic that discouraged agglo
 meration into a larger unit\, such as oil wealth\, proximity to white set
 tlement states\, Mandate/Trust status\, or location in a mountainous fron
 tier.
DURATION:PT1H15M
DTSTAMP:20260313T192204Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260318T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260313T192204Z
LOCATION;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0870ef-4078d128-0140-8383fed7-00007e8b:SSRI-Gros
 s Hall 270
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:PE/PI Workshop: Jack Paine (Emory)
